the line. A Puerto Rican mother steered two kids toward the picture
books. A boy at the next table copied a map of the world out of an at‐
las, tongue between his teeth, and across from him a boy a shade dark‐
er borrowed his eraser without asking. The librarian, spectacles on a
chain, stamped a book like she was signing a treaty. On the wall hung a
hand-lettered sign: FREE CONCERT, CONCOURSE PLAZA, SUNDAY,
ALL WELCOME.
    And the sound.
    It wasn’t harmony. Nobody in that room agreed on anything. It was
five languages and three arguments and a kid being shushed, and
somebody’s radio leaking a rumba through the wall, and it fit together
the way a block fits on a good day: not matching, just not fighting over
the same note.
    I went looking for the flat spot. The way you press a bruise. I
listened for the pressed-down place under the sound, the thumb on the
scale.
    There wasn’t one.
    I sat there, a man in a hoodie with a ginger ale, in a room that had
been dead for ninety years, and I realized I was close to crying and
couldn’t have said over what. It was something like homesickness for a
place I’d never been, except I had been, it was ten blocks from my
building, and nobody had ever told me it sounded like that.
    “You’re doing the thing.”
    Sofía’s hand was hovering over my shoulder, not touching. The read‐
ing room was the reading room again: fluorescent tubes, a water stain
on the ceiling, the snorer snoring. She had her laptop open before she
finished sitting down, because Sofía has two settings, asleep and pro‐
secuting.
    “Where’d you go?”
    “Nowhere. Library. I’m researching.”
    “You’re researching, and your face looks like somebody told you
your dog died.” She narrowed her eyes. “I’ll allow it. Also, hi. Eat
something. You look like a plant that’s given up.”



